#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124255 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20a6c0cf-04d4-5908-a03f-1adf491b9282" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "efccacfd-9595-5315-86c7-de9fefd8ed1f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5497" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $A$?\n\n1. $(\\lambda-2)^2(\\lambda-3)=0$\n2. $\\lambda^3-2\\lambda^2+3\\lambda=0$\n3. $\\lambda^3-4\\lambda + 7=0$" :question/worked-solution "According to the invertible matrix theorem, if $A$ is invertible, then $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $A$. As a result, $\\lambda=0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I could be the characteristic equation of $A$. Indeed, $\\lambda=0$ is not a root of the equation.\n- Equation II **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda=0$ is a root of the equation.\n- Equation III could be the characteristic equation of $A$. Indeed, $\\lambda=0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124256 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix and Rank-Nullity Theorems"}] :question/id #uuid "d7619d9b-e5d7-55e5-96a8-0d05b3532620" :question/math-academy-id "e-5498" :question/problem "If $B$ is a $4 \\times 4$ matrix and $\\textrm{rank}(B+4I) = 3$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\textrm{Null}(B+4I) = \\{\\mathbf{0} \\}$\n2. $B +4I$ is singular\n3. $\\lambda = -4$ is an eigenvalue of $B$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $4 \\times 4$ matrix $M$, we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n> $M$ is invertible $Col(M) = R^{4}; dim(Col(M)) = 4 = rank(M); Null(M) = {0}; dim(Null(M)) = 0 = nullity(M)$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(M) + \\textrm{nullity}(M) = 4\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem,\n$nullity(B + 4I)|= 4 - rank(B + 4I); = 4 - 3; = 1$.\nAs a result, $\\textrm{Null}(B+4I) \\ne \\{\\mathbf{0} \\}$.\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\textrm{nullity}(B+4I) \\neq 0$, we obtain that $B +4I$ must be singular.\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=B +4 I$ is singular, then $(B +4 I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $B$. As a result, $\\lambda=-4$ is an eigenvalue of $B$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124335 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "107369d4-980d-5d73-85ea-8fcf522b96f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8606" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix and $\\lambda=7$ is an eigenvalue of $A$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $(A-7I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ has a nonzero solution\n2. $\\textrm{rank}(A-7I) = 3$\n3. $\\textrm{Null}(A-7I) \\ne\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3 \\times 3$ matrix $M$, we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n> $M$ is invertible $Col(M) = R^{3}; dim(Col(M)) = 3 = rank(M); Null(M) = {0}; dim(Null(M)) = 0 = nullity(M)$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M = A- 7I$.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda=7$ is an eigenvalue of $A$, then the nonzero solutions of $(A-7I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ are eigenvectors of $A$, which are nonzero by definition. As a result, the matrix $M=A-7 I$ must be singular.\n- Statement II is false. Since $M=A-7I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n$\\textrm{rank}(A-7I) \\neq 3$.\n- Statement III is true. Since $M=A-7I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n$\\textrm{Null}(A-7I) \\neq \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124992 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20a6c0cf-04d4-5908-a03f-1adf491b9282" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "922f6dc5-18a2-5f92-b809-544a4bb67f1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122975} {:answer/id #uuid "9c7d4e4f-bcdd-5f67-9e2f-6c22c3b52fc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122976} {:answer/id #uuid "270749bc-e123-5a86-b43e-8245abff7179" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122977} {:answer/id #uuid "6050c1a6-7071-5021-8e89-f4014806d3a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122978} {:answer/id #uuid "7b2fbc0f-5bb6-5c0c-a8c7-e22488ed7934" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "270749bc-e123-5a86-b43e-8245abff7179" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122977} :answer-field/id #uuid "956bd7bf-0b11-54e4-a9dd-d3d307c70f5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122974}] :question/id #uuid "3be4d323-0e25-52c5-8eb8-5afe165a1c98" :question/math-academy-id "q-37523" :question/problem "A matrix $A$ has the characteristic equation $λ^{3} - λ^{2} - 6λ = 0$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $λ = 3$ is a root of the characteristic equation\n2. $λ = 0$ is an eigenvalue of $A$\n3. $A$ is invertible"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125081 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8696fd44-bd4e-5461-b7e5-7287f85bf436" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123509} {:answer/id #uuid "9b8c89a1-50a1-5165-96cc-15f61d335b91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123510} {:answer/id #uuid "0bedd711-a24a-5644-87f6-6015ccf4c7ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123511} {:answer/id #uuid "381a7f06-4af8-58d7-8b3d-44b4484df291" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123512} {:answer/id #uuid "63a7ed2b-095f-5b83-810c-92613569f55d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b8c89a1-50a1-5165-96cc-15f61d335b91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123510} :answer-field/id #uuid "deb36562-4d5d-5974-b8ea-ba2319a92e34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123508}] :question/id #uuid "c0e0c0ef-5198-55d8-9aeb-10b338580777" :question/math-academy-id "q-47568" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix and $λ = 3$ is an eigenvalue of $A$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $(A - 3I)x = 0$ has a nonzero solution\n2. $Col(A - 3I) = R^{2}$\n3. $nullity(A - 3I) = 0$"}]}
